---

<Layout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription} ogType="article" alternates={["en"]}>
    <SeoHead title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription} type="WebPage" />
    <script is:inline type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(articleJsonLd)} />
    <Nav />
    <Breadcrumb
        items={[
            { label: "Home", href: "/" },
            { label: "Guides", href: "/guides" },
            { label: "Loguru for Python" },
        ]}
    />

    <main>
        <header class="relative overflow-hidden border-b border-border pt-[19px]">
            <div
                class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0"
                aria-hidden="true"
                style="background-image: linear-gradient(to right, oklch(1 0 0 / 4%) 1px, transparent 1px), linear-gradient(to bottom, oklch(1 0 0 / 4%) 1px, transparent 1px); background-size: 60px 60px; mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse 78% 68% at 22% 26%, black, transparent);"
            >
            </div>
            <div class="relative mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 pb-16 pt-20 md:pb-20 md:pt-28">
                <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-fg-muted">
                    <span class="border border-primary/40 px-2 py-1 text-primary">Logging</span>
                    <span>7 min read</span>
                </div>
                <h1 class="mt-6 max-w-[18ch] font-display text-[clamp(40px,6.3vw,72px)] font-medium leading-[0.98] tracking-[-0.045em] text-fg">
                    Loguru for Python
                </h1>
                <p class="mt-7 max-w-[54ch] font-display text-[17px] leading-[1.6] text-fg-muted md:text-lg">
                    Install Loguru, log your first line, set it up for a real app, then send its logs to an observability tool with OpenTelemetry.
                </p>
                <div class="mt-7 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-5 gap-y-2 font-mono text-[10px] text-fg-muted">
                    <a href={authorUrl} rel="noopener" class="flex items-center gap-2 transition-colors hover:text-fg">
                        <img src={author.avatar} alt="" width="20" height="20" class="h-5 w-5 rounded-full ring-1 ring-border" />
                        <span>By {author.name}</span>
                    </a>
                    <time datetime="2026-10-22">Updated {updatedLabel}</time>
                </div>
            </div>
        </header>

        <nav aria-label="Guide sections" class="border-b border-border bg-bg-deep">
            <div class="mx-auto flex max-w-6xl gap-6 overflow-x-auto px-6 py-4 font-mono text-[10px] whitespace-nowrap text-fg-muted">
                {sectionNav.map((h) => (
                    <a href={`#${h.slug}`} class="transition-colors hover:text-primary">{h.text}</a>
                ))}
            </div>
        </nav>

        <div class="mx-auto grid max-w-6xl gap-12 px-6 py-16 md:py-24 lg:grid-cols-[14rem_minmax(0,1fr)] lg:gap-16">
            <aside class="hidden lg:block">
                <TableOfContents headings={toc} class="sticky top-28" />
            </aside>

            <article class="loguru-guide min-w-0">
                <section class="guide-lead">
                    <p>
                        Your Python app needs logs you can read when something breaks, and the standard <code>logging</code> module makes you wire up handlers and formatters before you get a rotating log file or JSON lines. <strong>Loguru</strong> is a popular logging library for Python that takes almost no setup: you import one ready-made <code>logger</code> and call it. In production, a log file without rotation grows until the disk is full, and lines from requests handled at the same time interleave with nothing to tell them apart. This guide takes you from the first line to JSON log files and an observability tool that shows which request wrote each line.
                    </p>

                    <div class="guide-takeaways">
                        <p class="guide-kicker">Key takeaways</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><code>from loguru import logger</code> is the whole setup; <code>logger.add()</code> adds sinks such as files.</li>
                            <li><code>rotation</code> and <code>retention</code> on a file sink keep log files from filling the disk.</li>
                            <li><code>serialize=True</code> writes JSON lines that log search tools can filter field by field.</li>
                            <li>Add production sinks with <code>diagnose=False</code> so tracebacks don't print passwords and tokens.</li>
                            <li>In a package other people install, log with the standard <code>logging</code> module; use Loguru in your own apps.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section id="get-started" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow"><span>01</span> · Basics</p>
                    <h2>Get started with Loguru</h2>

                    <p>Install Loguru with pip, then import <code>logger</code> and start logging. Loguru prints colored lines to your terminal out of the box:</p>
                    <pre><code>{installCommand}</code></pre>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(quickstartCode, "python-logging")} /></pre>
                    <pre class="pre-output"><code set:html={highlight(quickstartOutput, "loguru-output")} /></pre>

                    <div class="anatomy" role="img" aria-label="The parts of a Loguru line: the time, the level INFO, the place in your code __main__:<module>:4, and your message.">
                        <div class="anatomy__line" aria-hidden="true">
                            {anatomy.map(([text, field, body], index) => (
                                <>
                                    {index > 0 && <span class="anatomy__sep">{index === 3 ? "-" : "|"}</span>}
                                    <div class="anatomy__col">
                                        <span class="anatomy__part">{text}</span>
                                        <div class="anatomy__key"><code>{field}</code><span>{body}</span></div>
                                    </div>
                                </>
                            ))}
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <p>
                        A <code>{"{}"}</code> in the message is filled with the next argument, and <code>{"{percent}"}</code> with the argument named <code>percent</code>.
                    </p>
                </section>

                <section id="configure" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow"><span>02</span> · Configure</p>
                    <h2>Levels, files and formats</h2>
                    <p>
                        Loguru calls each place it writes logs to a <strong>sink</strong>. A fresh <code>logger</code> has one sink, your terminal. <code>logger.add()</code> adds a sink and <code>logger.remove()</code> takes sinks away.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="set-level" class="scroll-mt-24">Set the log level</h3>
                    <p>The terminal sink shows every level from <code>DEBUG</code> up. To show less, remove it and add it back with the level you want:</p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(setLevelCode, "python-logging")} /></pre>
                    <pre class="pre-output"><code set:html={highlight(setLevelOutput, "loguru-output")} /></pre>
                    <p>
                        <code>logger.remove()</code> with no arguments removes every sink. To change the level without touching code, start the app with the environment variable <code>LOGURU_LEVEL=INFO</code>.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="levels" class="scroll-mt-24">Loguru levels</h3>
                    <p>
                        Each level has a number. A sink shows its own level and every level with a higher number. The numbers match Python's standard <code>logging</code> module, plus <code>TRACE</code> and <code>SUCCESS</code>.
                    </p>
                    <div class="guide-table">
                        <table>
                            <thead>
                                <tr><th>Level</th><th>No.</th><th>Method</th></tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                {levels.map((l) => (
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><span class={`level level--${l.name.toLowerCase()}`}>{l.name}</span></td>
                                        <td>{l.no}</td>
                                        <td><code>{l.method}</code></td>
                                    </tr>
                                ))}
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                    <p>To add your own level, give it a name and a number. 35 puts it between <code>WARNING</code> and <code>ERROR</code>:</p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(customLevelCode, "python-logging")} /></pre>
                    <pre class="pre-output"><code set:html={highlight(customLevelOutput, "loguru-output")} /></pre>

                    <h3 id="files" class="scroll-mt-24">Write logs to a file</h3>
                    <p>Pass a file path to <code>logger.add()</code>. Loguru can also start a new file when the current one gets big, delete old files and compress them:</p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(fileCode, "python-logging")} /></pre>
                    <p>
                        <code>rotation</code> also takes a time of day, such as <code>"00:00"</code> for a new file every midnight, or an interval such as <code>"1 week"</code>.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="format" class="scroll-mt-24">Change the format</h3>
                    <p>
                        The <code>format</code> argument sets what each line shows. Names in braces are filled in for every line, and tags like <code>&lt;green&gt;</code> add color. Loguru leaves the colors out when it writes to a file.
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(formatCode, "python-logging")} /></pre>
                    <pre class="pre-output"><code set:html={highlight(formatOutput, "loguru-output")} /></pre>
                </section>

                <section id="structured" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow"><span>03</span> · Structure</p>
                    <h2>JSON logs and context</h2>

                    <h3 id="json" class="scroll-mt-24">JSON output with serialize=True</h3>
                    <p>Log search tools can filter JSON field by field, which plain text does not allow. Add a sink with <code>serialize=True</code> and each line comes out as a JSON object with everything Loguru knows about the call (trimmed here):</p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(serializeCode, "python-logging")} /></pre>
                    <pre class="pre-output"><code set:html={highlight(serializeOutput, "json")} /></pre>
                    <p>
                        If you send your logs to an observability tool, the <a href="#opentelemetry">OpenTelemetry setup</a> below sends these fields for you.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="context" class="scroll-mt-24">Add context with bind() and contextualize()</h3>
                    <p>
                        Context is extra data that travels with a log line, such as a user name or a request ID. Loguru keeps it in a dict called <code>extra</code> and gives you two ways to fill it:
                    </p>
                    <ul class="guide-list">
                        <li><code>logger.bind()</code> returns a copy of the logger with fixed values. Lines logged through the copy carry them, and the plain <code>logger</code> stays as it was.</li>
                        <li><code>logger.contextualize()</code> adds values to every line logged inside a <code>with</code> block, including lines from the functions it calls.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(contextCode, "python-logging")} /></pre>
                    <pre class="pre-output"><code set:html={highlight(contextOutput, "loguru-output")} /></pre>
                    <p>
                        <code>contextualize()</code> values stay with the thread or asyncio task that set them, so in a web app, requests handled at the same time keep their own request IDs.
                    </p>
                </section>

                <section id="exceptions" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow"><span>04</span> · Errors</p>
                    <h2>Log exceptions with their variables</h2>
                    <p>
                        <code>@logger.catch</code> logs any exception the function raises, with its traceback, and lets the program carry on. Loguru's tracebacks also show the value of each variable in them:
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(catchCode, "python-logging")} /></pre>
                    <pre class="pre-output"><code set:html={highlight(catchOutput, "loguru-output")} /></pre>
                    <p>
                        Inside an <code>except</code> block, <code>logger.exception("Charge failed")</code> does the same for the exception you caught.
                    </p>
                    <div class="guide-callout">
                        <p class="guide-kicker">Hide variable values in production</p>
                        <p>
                            They can contain passwords, tokens and customer data. Add production sinks with <code>diagnose=False</code>, or start the app with <code>LOGURU_DIAGNOSE=NO</code> for the default terminal sink.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section id="stdlib" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow"><span>05</span> · Standard library</p>
                    <h2>Loguru and the logging module</h2>

                    <h3 id="intercept" class="scroll-mt-24"><span class="sys-dot" style={`--sys: ${systems.stdlib.color}`}></span>Send library logs through Loguru</h3>
                    <p>
                        Libraries like requests, SQLAlchemy and uvicorn log with the standard <code>logging</code> module, so their lines skip your Loguru sinks. The Loguru docs include a small handler that forwards them. Save it as <code>intercept.py</code>:
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(interceptCode, "python-logging")} /></pre>
                    <p>Import it once, first thing at startup, and lines from Loguru and from libraries come out of the same sinks:</p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(interceptUsageCode, "python-logging")} /></pre>
                    <pre class="pre-output"><code set:html={highlight(interceptUsageOutput, "loguru-output")} /></pre>

                    <h3 id="comparison" class="scroll-mt-24">Loguru vs the logging module</h3>
                    <div class="guide-table">
                        <table>
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Task</th>
                                    <th><span class="sys-badge" style={`--sys: ${systems.loguru.color}`}>Loguru</span></th>
                                    <th><span class="sys-badge" style={`--sys: ${systems.stdlib.color}`}>logging</span></th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                {comparison.map(([task, loguru, stdlib]) => (
                                    <tr><td>{task}</td><td set:html={loguru} /><td set:html={stdlib} /></tr>
                                ))}
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                    <p>
                        Writing a package that other people install? Log with the standard <code>logging</code> module there, so the apps that use it decide where its logs go. Use Loguru in your own apps.
                    </p>
                </section>

                <section id="opentelemetry" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow"><span>06</span> · OpenTelemetry</p>
                    <h2>Send Loguru logs to OpenTelemetry</h2>
                    <p>
                        OpenTelemetry is an open standard for sending logs, traces and metrics to an observability tool. With the setup below, your Loguru lines go to any backend that accepts OTLP, the OpenTelemetry protocol, and each line carries the ID of the request that wrote it. The example sends to Maple.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        OpenTelemetry's Python SDK reads logs from the standard <code>logging</code> module, not from Loguru. The setup adds one Loguru sink, a bridge, that passes each line on:
                    </p>

                    <figure class="flow" aria-label="The path of a log line. Your code calls the Loguru logger, which writes to the terminal and files and to otel_sink, the bridge. otel_sink hands the line to loguru.otel, a logging logger, whose LoggingHandler adds the trace ID and passes it to the LoggerProvider, which exports it to Maple or any OTLP backend.">
                        <svg viewBox="0 0 760 216" aria-hidden="true">
                            <defs>
                                {["loguru", "stdlib", "otel", "primary"].map((k) => (
                                    <marker id={`flow-head-${k}`} viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
                                        <path d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z" class={`flow__head flow__head--${k}`} />
                                    </marker>
                                ))}
                            </defs>

                            <rect x="0" y="24" width="150" height="64" rx="8" class="flow__box flow__box--loguru" />
                            <text x="75" y="51" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__title">Your code</text>
                            <text x="75" y="71" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__mono flow__mono--loguru">logger.info()</text>
                            <rect x="203" y="24" width="150" height="64" rx="8" class="flow__box flow__box--loguru flow__box--strong" />
                            <text x="278" y="51" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__title">Loguru logger</text>
                            <text x="278" y="71" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__mono flow__mono--loguru">every sink</text>
                            <rect x="406" y="24" width="150" height="64" rx="8" class="flow__box flow__box--loguru" />
                            <text x="481" y="51" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__title">otel_sink</text>
                            <text x="481" y="71" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__mono flow__mono--loguru">the bridge</text>
                            <rect x="610" y="24" width="150" height="64" rx="8" class="flow__box flow__box--stdlib" />
                            <text x="685" y="51" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__title">loguru.otel</text>
                            <text x="685" y="71" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__mono flow__mono--stdlib">a logging logger</text>
                            <rect x="0" y="150" width="150" height="64" rx="8" class="flow__box flow__box--loguru" />
                            <text x="75" y="177" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__title">Terminal, files</text>
                            <text x="75" y="197" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__mono flow__mono--loguru">your other sinks</text>
                            <rect x="203" y="150" width="150" height="64" rx="8" class="flow__box flow__box--primary" />
                            <text x="278" y="177" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__title">Maple</text>
                            <text x="278" y="197" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__mono">or any OTLP backend</text>
                            <rect x="406" y="150" width="150" height="64" rx="8" class="flow__box flow__box--otel" />
                            <text x="481" y="177" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__title">LoggerProvider</text>
                            <text x="481" y="197" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__mono flow__mono--otel">exports in batches</text>
                            <rect x="610" y="150" width="150" height="64" rx="8" class="flow__box flow__box--otel" />
                            <text x="685" y="177" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__title">LoggingHandler</text>
                            <text x="685" y="197" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__mono flow__mono--otel">+ trace ID</text>
                            <line x1="150" y1="56" x2="197" y2="56" marker-end="url(#flow-head-loguru)" class="flow__line flow__line--loguru" />
                            <line x1="353" y1="56" x2="400" y2="56" marker-end="url(#flow-head-loguru)" class="flow__line flow__line--loguru" />
                            <line x1="556" y1="56" x2="604" y2="56" marker-end="url(#flow-head-stdlib)" class="flow__line flow__line--stdlib" />
                            <line x1="685" y1="88" x2="685" y2="144" marker-end="url(#flow-head-stdlib)" class="flow__line flow__line--stdlib" />
                            <line x1="610" y1="182" x2="562" y2="182" marker-end="url(#flow-head-otel)" class="flow__line flow__line--otel" />
                            <line x1="406" y1="182" x2="359" y2="182" marker-end="url(#flow-head-primary)" class="flow__line flow__line--primary" />
                            <line x1="240" y1="88" x2="120" y2="144" marker-end="url(#flow-head-loguru)" class="flow__line flow__line--loguru" />
                        </svg>
                    </figure>

                    <h3 id="otel-setup" class="scroll-mt-24"><span class="sys-dot" style={`--sys: ${systems.otel.color}`}></span>Install and configure</h3>
                    <pre><code>{otelInstall}</code></pre>
                    <p>
                        Create <code>telemetry.py</code>. It names your service, points OpenTelemetry at your backend, and adds the bridge. The traces part is there for the request IDs: a <strong>trace</strong> records one request, and every line logged during it carries the trace's ID.
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(telemetryCode, "python-logging")} /></pre>
                    <p>
                        For Maple, replace <code>YOUR_INGEST_KEY</code> with the private ingest key from the <strong>Ingestion</strong> page in Maple's settings; organizations in the EU region use <code>https://ingest.eu.maple.dev</code>. For another backend, change <code>ENDPOINT</code> and <code>HEADERS</code>: a local OpenTelemetry Collector, for example, listens on <code>http://localhost:4318</code> and needs no header.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="otel-run" class="scroll-mt-24">Run it</h3>
                    <p>
                        Import <code>telemetry</code> once, before the first log call. A trace is made of <strong>spans</strong>, one per step of the request. Web framework instrumentation starts a span for each request; this example starts one by hand:
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(otelAppCode, "python-logging")} /></pre>
                    <pre class="pre-output"><code set:html={highlight(otelRun, "loguru-output")} /></pre>
                    <p>
                        The terminal shows the four lines. The backend receives them too, all with the trace ID of <code>POST /checkout</code>, and <code>order_id</code> from <code>bind()</code> arrives as a field on its line. The SDK sends lines in batches about once a second and sends the rest when the program exits.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Using <code>intercept.py</code>? Import it before <code>telemetry</code>, and library logs reach the backend through the same bridge. The exporter's HTTP client then logs each upload to your terminal at <code>DEBUG</code>; <code>logging.getLogger("urllib3").setLevel(logging.WARNING)</code> hides those lines.
                    </p>
                    <div class="guide-callout">
                        <p class="guide-kicker">Two ways to lose the request ID</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Adding <code>otel_sink</code> with <code>enqueue=True</code>, Loguru's option to write from a background thread. That thread is outside the request, so the trace ID arrives as all zeros.</li>
                            <li>A <code>bind()</code> key that <code>logging</code> already uses, such as <code>name</code>, <code>module</code> or <code>message</code>. <code>logging</code> rejects it and the line never reaches the backend. Use names like <code>user_name</code>.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>

                    <h3 id="maple" class="scroll-mt-24">See the logs in Maple</h3>
                    <ol class="guide-steps">
                        <li>
                            <span>01</span>
                            <div><h4>Find the lines</h4><p>Open <strong>Logs</strong> and pick your service under <strong>Service</strong> in the sidebar. Paste a trace ID into <strong>Search</strong> to list every line from one request.</p>
                                <figure class="guide-figure"><img src="/screenshots/guides/loguru-01-logs.webp" data-zoomable alt="The Logs page filtered to the checkout service and one trace ID, listing the four lines one POST /checkout request wrote through Loguru: two INFO, one WARN and one SUCCESS." width="2362" height="1105" loading="lazy" /></figure></div>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <span>02</span>
                            <div><h4>Open a line</h4><p>Click a row, then <strong>Open detail</strong>. Bound values such as <code>order_id</code> and the file, function and line that logged it are under <strong>Log Attributes</strong>. The <code>trace:</code> link opens the request the line belongs to.</p>
                                <figure class="guide-figure"><img src="/screenshots/guides/loguru-02-log-detail.webp" data-zoomable alt="The log detail sheet for Charging card: the trace link and span ID in the meta strip, and Log Attributes code.file.path, code.function.name, code.line.number and order_id." width="1342" height="866" loading="lazy" /></figure></div>
                        </li>
                    </ol>

                    <div class="guide-maple">
                        <p class="guide-kicker">Loguru logs in Maple</p>
                        <h3>Send your first Loguru line</h3>
                        <p>For traces and metrics from the same app, see the Python guide.</p>
                        <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
                            <a href="/docs/guides/instrumentation-python" class="guide-button guide-button--primary">Set up Python with Maple</a>
                            <a href="/docs/explore/logs" class="guide-button">Logs docs</a>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="guide-sources">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow">Sources and further reading</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><a href="https://loguru.readthedocs.io/en/stable/api/logger.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Loguru docs: the logger API</a></li>
                        <li><a href="https://loguru.readthedocs.io/en/stable/overview.html#entirely-compatible-with-standard-logging" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Loguru docs: compatibility with standard logging</a></li>
                        <li><a href="https://pypi.org/project/loguru/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PyPI: loguru</a></li>
                        <li><a href="https://pypi.org/project/opentelemetry-instrumentation-logging/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PyPI: opentelemetry-instrumentation-logging</a></li>
                        <li><a href="/docs/guides/instrumentation-python">Maple docs: Python instrumentation</a></li>
                        <li><a href="/docs/explore/logs">Maple docs: Logs</a></li>
                    </ul>
                </section>
            </article>
        </div>
    </main>

